# operator-precedence — the infix expression grammar shipped in 0.22.0.
#
# Before 0.22.0 a library that wanted `1 + 2 * 3` had to declare the shape
# itself, one function per operator, and comparison was a single
# non-associative step. Now a single `any` capture takes the whole
# expression and the engine builds the tree, with conventional precedence
# and left associativity.
#
# The library is deliberately tiny — three functions — because the point
# is what the ENGINE does with the captured expression, not what the
# library does with it. Two views of every value are printed:
#
#   ${value}                 -> the SOURCE TEXT the author wrote
#   ${context.vars[name]}    -> the EVALUATED result
#
# Those two faces of one capture are the reason the precedence is visible
# at all: the left column is what you typed, the right column is what it
# means. See docs/language-reference.md#operator-precedence.
extension calc

context
    vars {}
end

# `let NAME = EXPR` — capture a whole expression as one value.
function let
    arg literal "let"
    arg capture name ident
    arg literal "="
    arg capture value any
    set context.vars[name] value
    write `let ${name} = ${value}
`
end

# `show NAME` — read the evaluated value back out of context.
#
# A separate function on purpose: a `set` lands for the NEXT statement, so
# a function's own `write` cannot see its own mutation.
function show
    arg literal "show"
    arg capture name ident
    write `      ${name} == ${context.vars[name]}
`
end

# `note TEXT` — a section heading, so the golden reads as a document.
function note
    arg literal "note"
    arg capture text tail
    write `
${text}
`
end
